This version follows a simple LIS-style dynamic programming pattern. Words are sorted by length, and `dp[i]` stores the longest chain ending at `words[i]`. A two-pointer check decides whether an earlier word can become the current word by adding one character.

Dry run with `a`, `ba`, `bda`: `a` gets `1`, `ba` gets `2`, and `bda` gets `3` because each word is a valid predecessor of the next one.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int longestStrChain(String[] words) {
        if (words == null || words.length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        Arrays.sort(words, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a.length(), b.length()));
        int[] dp = new int[words.length];
        Arrays.fill(dp, 1);

        int answer = 1;

        for (int i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (isPredecessor(words[j], words[i])) {
                    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);
                }
            }
            answer = Math.max(answer, dp[i]);
        }

        return answer;
    }

    private boolean isPredecessor(String shorter, String longer) {
        if (longer.length() != shorter.length() + 1) {
            return false;
        }

        int shortIndex = 0;
        int longIndex = 0;

        while (shortIndex < shorter.length() && longIndex < longer.length()) {
            if (shorter.charAt(shortIndex) == longer.charAt(longIndex)) {
                shortIndex++;
            }
            longIndex++;
        }

        return shortIndex == shorter.length();
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2 * L)`, because every pair may be checked and a check takes `O(L)` time.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the DP array. This version changes the input array by sorting it.

Each word can be treated as a node. Removing one character from a word gives possible predecessor nodes, so every edge goes from a shorter word to a longer word. Since lengths always increase, the graph has no cycles.

The queue starts with words that have no predecessor. When a word is processed, it improves the chain length of each longer word connected to it. This is a little more code than a direct map DP, but it makes the graph idea clear.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public int LongestStringChain(string[] words)
    {
        if (words == null || words.Length == 0)
        {
            return 0;
        }

        var wordSet = new HashSet<string>(words);
        var graph = new Dictionary<string, List<string>>();
        var indegree = new Dictionary<string, int>();
        var distance = new Dictionary<string, int>();

        foreach (string word in words)
        {
            if (!indegree.ContainsKey(word))
            {
                indegree[word] = 0;
                distance[word] = 1;
                graph[word] = new List<string>();
            }
        }

        foreach (string word in words)
        {
            var seenPredecessors = new HashSet<string>();

            for (int i = 0; i < word.Length; i++)
            {
                string predecessor = word.Remove(i, 1);

                if (wordSet.Contains(predecessor) && seenPredecessors.Add(predecessor))
                {
                    graph[predecessor].Add(word);
                    indegree[word]++;
                }
            }
        }

        var queue = new Queue<string>();
        foreach (var entry in indegree)
        {
            if (entry.Value == 0)
            {
                queue.Enqueue(entry.Key);
            }
        }

        int answer = 1;

        while (queue.Count > 0)
        {
            string current = queue.Dequeue();

            foreach (string next in graph[current])
            {
                distance[next] = Math.Max(distance[next], distance[current] + 1);
                answer = Math.Max(answer, distance[next]);
                indegree[next]--;

                if (indegree[next] == 0)
                {
                    queue.Enqueue(next);
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n * L^2)`, mainly because each deletion creates a new string. The queue and edge processing add `O(n * L)` work.

Space complexity: `O(n * L)` for the word graph and its stored edges. Duplicate predecessor edges are removed with a set.

This uses buckets by word length instead of sorting the whole array. A word of length `k` can only connect to words of length `k - 1`, so the code works from longer buckets down to shorter ones.

For each longer word, deleting one character creates a possible predecessor. If that predecessor exists, its chain value is updated from the current word's value. The set in each bucket also avoids checking unrelated lengths.

A quick dry run for `a`, `ba`, `bda`: processing `bda` can update `ba` to `2`, then processing `ba` can update `a` to `3` in reverse form. The final answer is still the longest chain length.

```javascript
class Solution {
    longestStringChain(words) {
        if (words.length === 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        const buckets = Array.from({ length: 17 }, () => new Set());
        const chain = new Map();

        for (const word of words) {
            buckets[word.length].add(word);
            chain.set(word, 1);
        }

        let answer = 1;

        for (let length = 16; length >= 2; length--) {
            for (const word of buckets[length]) {
                const currentLength = chain.get(word);

                for (let i = 0; i < word.length; i++) {
                    const predecessor = word.slice(0, i) + word.slice(i + 1);

                    if (buckets[length - 1].has(predecessor)) {
                        const oldValue = chain.get(predecessor);
                        const newValue = Math.max(oldValue, currentLength + 1);
                        chain.set(predecessor, newValue);
                        answer = Math.max(answer, newValue);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n * L^2)`, since each word produces up to `L` shortened strings and each string operation can take `O(L)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the buckets and chain map. The input array itself is not modified.

Instead of sorting, this version starts from every word and recursively removes one character. A set tells whether the shortened word exists. The memo table keeps the answer for each word, so the same word is not explored again.

The empty string is not needed in the set. If a one-letter word is processed, all its shortened candidates simply miss the set and its chain remains `1`.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <unordered_set>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int LongestFrom(const string& word,
                    const unordered_set<string>& wordSet,
                    unordered_map<string, int>& memo) {
        auto saved = memo.find(word);
        if (saved != memo.end()) {
            return saved->second;
        }

        int best = 1;

        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(word.size()); i++) {
            string predecessor = word.substr(0, i) + word.substr(i + 1);

            if (wordSet.count(predecessor)) {
                best = max(best, LongestFrom(predecessor, wordSet, memo) + 1);
            }
        }

        memo[word] = best;
        return best;
    }

    int longestStrChain(vector<string>& words) {
        if (words.empty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        unordered_set<string> wordSet(words.begin(), words.end());
        unordered_map<string, int> memo;
        int answer = 0;

        for (const string& word : words) {
            answer = max(answer, LongestFrom(word, wordSet, memo));
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * L^2)` in the usual string-copy model. Each word is solved once, and its shortened strings take up to `O(L^2)` total to create.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the set, memo table, and recursion stack. The input array is not changed.

Sorting by word length makes the smaller words ready before the longer ones. For each word, the code removes one character at a time and checks whether the result was already seen. The best chain for that shorter word can then be extended.

For example, from `bdca`, it checks `dca`, `bca`, `bda`, and `bdc`. Only words that are actually in the input can continue the chain.

```python
class Solution:
    def longestStrChain(self, words):
        words.sort(key=len)
        chain_length = {}
        answer = 0

        for word in words:
            best_for_word = 1

            for i in range(len(word)):
                predecessor = word[:i] + word[i + 1:]
                if predecessor in chain_length:
                    best_for_word = max(
                        best_for_word,
                        chain_length[predecessor] + 1
                    )

            chain_length[word] = best_for_word
            answer = max(answer, best_for_word)

        return answer
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n + n * L^2)`, where `n` is the number of words and `L` is the maximum word length. Creating each shortened string can take `O(L)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the map, not counting the input list.
